Primary Function (Main Purpose) of Streams

Streams in Java 8 are used to process data from collections efficiently, clearly, and functionally 
— without writing boilerplate loops.

Restrictions / Rules / Limitations of Streams:
Stream can be consumed only once - Once a stream is operated on (with a terminal operation),
 you cannot reuse it.

Streams don’t store data - They don’t modify or hold the original data; they just process it temporarily. 

Streams work with functional interfaces - You use lambda expressions or method references with Stream operations:
map(Function)
filter(Predicate)
forEach(Consumer)
They have rules: single-use, lazy, non-storing data, and rely on functional interfaces.

| Stream Functionality              | Description                        | Example                           |
| --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| **Transformation (map)**          | Convert elements into another form | Convert lowercase to uppercase    |
| **Filtering (filter)**            | Select only certain elements       | Keep only names starting with "S" |
| **Sorting (sorted)**              | Arrange data                       | Sort numbers ascending            |
| **Aggregation (reduce)**          | Combine elements into one result   | Sum all numbers                   |
| **Collecting (collect / toList)** | Gather results into a collection   | Convert stream to List or Set     |
| **Iteration (forEach)**           | Perform action on each element     | Print each item                   |


after you call .stream(), you can chain stream operations, 
and those operations fall into two types — intermediate and terminal.

You don’t have to call .map() or .filter() specifically —
you can use any intermediate operations that make sense for your data —
but the pipeline must always end with a terminal operation like .toList() or .forEach() to actually execute.

Two types of operations on streams
🟦 Intermediate operations

These transform or filter the stream and return another Stream.
They are lazy — they don’t execute immediately

| Operation            | Purpose                  | Uses Functional Interface |
| -------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------- |
| `.map(Function)`     | Transform each element   | Function                  |
| `.filter(Predicate)` | Keep matching elements   | Predicate                 |
| `.sorted()`          | Sort elements            | Comparator                |
| `.distinct()`        | Remove duplicates        | —                         |
| `.limit(n)`          | Take first n elements    | —                         |
| `.skip(n)`           | Skip first n elements    | —                         |
| `.peek(Consumer)`    | Debug / inspect elements | Consumer                  |



Terminal operations

These trigger the stream pipeline —
they consume the stream and produce a result (list, count, print, etc.).

| Operation                       | Purpose                                | Uses Functional Interface |
| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| `.forEach(Consumer)`            | Perform action (e.g. print)            | Consumer                  |
| `.collect(Collectors.toList())` | Collect results into a list            | Collector                 |
| `.toList()` *(Java 16+)*        | Collect results into an immutable list | —                         |
| `.count()`                      | Count elements                         | —                         |
| `.findFirst()`                  | Return first element                   | —                         |
| `.findAny()`                    | Return any element                     | —                         |
| `.anyMatch(Predicate)`          | Check if any element matches           | Predicate                 |
| `.allMatch(Predicate)`          | Check if all elements match            | Predicate                 |
| `.noneMatch(Predicate)`         | Check if none match                    | Predicate                 |
| `.reduce()`                     | Combine elements into one result       | BinaryOperator            |
